// Practice quiz: Standard error responses with ProblemDetail
// Minimum release, November 2026: author-checked, no independent review. Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.

$CATEGORY: spring-boot/validation-errors-and-testing/standard-error-responses-with-problemdetail

::o1 members::Which of these is a standard RFC 9457 problem details member? {
~stackTrace#Not quite. It is not standard, and should not be sent.
~exceptionClass#Not quite. It is not standard.
~sqlState#Not quite. It is not standard.
=title#Correct. Others are type, status, detail and instance.
}

::o1 extras::How do you add an extra value to a ProblemDetail? {
~setType with a number#Not quite. type is a URI.
=setProperty#Correct. It adds a non-standard member.
~Putting it in the status#Not quite. Status is the HTTP code.
~A second response body#Not quite. A response has one body.
}

::o2 enable::Which property turns on problem details for Spring MVC framework exceptions? {
=spring.mvc.problemdetails.enabled#Correct. It adds handling for framework exceptions.
~server.error.include-stacktrace#Not quite. That is about stack traces.
~spring.jpa.show-sql#Not quite. That is SQL logging.
~spring.profiles.active#Not quite. That activates profiles.
}

::o2 validation body::With problem details on, what is the status of a default validation failure? {
~200#Not quite. It is still a failure.
~500#Not quite. It is a client error.
=400#Correct. Only the format changes.
~422 always#Not quite. The framework default is 400.
}

::o3 why standard::Why does one standard error shape help API clients? {
~It removes all errors#Not quite. It only describes them.
~It removes the need for HTTP status#Not quite. Status is still there.
~It hides errors from logs#Not quite. Logs are separate.
=They can parse every error with the same code#Correct. There are no per-endpoint special cases.
}

